
<% content_for :title, "On Psychosis | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="psychosis-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="psychosis-hero">
    <div class="psychosis-hero-content">

      <p class="psychosis-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PSYCHOSIS
      </p>

      <p class="psychosis-hero-number">
        07 / ON PSYCHOSIS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>ON</span>
        <span>PSYCHOSIS<span class="psychosis-dot">.</span></span>
      </h1>

      <p class="psychosis-hero-tagline">
        WHEN YOUR MIND<br>
        BECOMES YOUR<br>
        ENTIRE REALITY.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- OPENING -->
  <section class="psychosis-opening" id="psychosis-story">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">01 / THE EXPERIENCE</p>

      <h2>
        IT WAS<br>
        <span>SPINNING</span><br>
        ON ITS OWN.
      </h2>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p class="psychosis-opening-line">
          It was spinning on its own.
          <em>Round and round.</em>
        </p>

        <p>
          It was playing music on its own—a screamo rendition
          of "Amazing Grace".
          <em>Interesting choice</em>.
        </p>

        <p>
          They were talking about me outside the building.
          I stopped when I read a book. They were knocking on
          doors, asking if my neighbors would participate in
          an experiment. Then they were watching me. They were
          narrating everything I was doing. I checked my
          apartment for cameras and microphones as they
          mocked me, laughing at my efforts to find out
          where this was coming from.
        </p>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 911 -->
  <section class="psychosis-scene psychosis-scene-pink">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">02 / THE CALL</p>

      <h2>911.</h2>

      <div class="psychosis-dialogue">

        <p class="psychosis-dialogue-label">
          ME / ON THE PHONE
        </p>

        <p class="psychosis-dialogue-big">
          "THEY'RE WATCHING ME.<br>
          THEY'RE TALKING ABOUT ME.<br>
          THEY'RE IN MY APARTMENT!"
        </p>

        <p class="psychosis-dialogue-small">
          "Who?"
        </p>

        <p class="psychosis-dialogue-answer">
          "....They!!"
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">
        <p>
          A police officer showed up at my door.
          How he got in the building I had no idea. He asked
          me if I wanted to leave with him. I knew I would
          be arrested, never to see Banjo, my family, or my
          friends ever again—to be locked away for a crime
          I didn't even know I had committed.
          What else could've warranted this?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- THE NIGHT -->
  <section class="psychosis-scene psychosis-scene-dark">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">03 / THE NIGHT</p>

      <h2>
        NOTHING<br>
        <span>WAS REAL.</span><br>
        EVERYTHING<br>
        FELT REAL.
      </h2>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          Sweat coming out of every pore, music played out
          of my white noise machine. Things on my wall
          morphed into terrifying faces. Curtains moved
          in a wind despite the windows were locked tight.
          The city outside was blank—the voices were the
          only thing that was the focal point.
          My brain was swimming.
        </p>

        <p>
          The four of them were throwing around ideas of
          how to torture me, taking turns at poking my skin
          with hot knives and laughing at my pain. They
          sang; they scientifically spoke about what was
          going on. One of them started to move my phone
          off my bed, as they had done with my vape.
          They had moved shoes across my floor already,
          what was one more thing?
        </p>

      </div>

      <blockquote class="psychosis-pullquote">
        "SHE NEEDS A JOB.<br>
        LEAVE THAT.<br>
        WHAT IS A CHEMIST<br>
        WITHOUT THEIR<br>
        CHEMISTRY SET?"
      </blockquote>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          Their leader said, "She needs a job. Leave that.
          What is a chemist without their chemistry set?"
          One of them was named David. He was the least
          experienced. His knives hurt the least, but
          they were still fiery hot.
        </p>

        <p>
          It smelled of sulfur. I sat in a puddle of my
          own sweat. They communicated with me through
          a Bluetooth headset, giving me instructions
          for later. They said they were from the
          Canadian government, and I was being punished
          for a DUI.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- SLEEP -->
  <section class="psychosis-sleep">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">04 / NO SLEEP</p>

      <p class="psychosis-sleep-lead">
        THREE DAYS.
      </p>

      <h2>
        NO<br>
        <span>SLEEP.</span>
      </h2>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          By sunrise, they hadn't let up. At 8am, they
          said their shift was over, and the day shift
          would take over. I hadn't slept in three days.
          By then, I was so exhausted I passed out
          for a few hours.
        </p>

        <p>
          Eventually, I began to wake up and tried to
          live as normal. I started to apply for jobs
          with two women from previous night narrating
          everything I was doing, reading what I wrote,
          mocking me, and speaking as if they were sending
          a report to a manager. I wondered who the
          manager was as I groggily continued through
          the day.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- RECORD PLAYER -->
  <section class="psychosis-scene psychosis-scene-purple">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">05 / THE RECORD PLAYER</p>

      <div class="psychosis-command">
        <span class="psychosis-command-index">
          THE VOICE
        </span>

        <p>
          "PUT YOUR PHONE<br>
          ON THE RECORD<br>
          PLAYER."
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          "Put your phone on the record player."
          The instruction came clear.
          "And don't look." I did as told,
          but I watched in a mirror.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-warning">
        "DO NOT LOOK!"
      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          I didn't look right then, but I hear a
          jolt-zap noise and turned slightly to see
          what looked like a small bolt of lightning
          come from the spinning record player
          and my cell phone.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- REACHING OUT -->
  <section class="psychosis-scene psychosis-scene-yellow">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">06 / REACHING OUT</p>

      <h2>
        I HAD<br>
        <span>NEVER BEEN</span><br>
        MORE SCARED.
      </h2>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          Later, I remembered I had another phone.
          They had established that the bathroom was
          a safe zone for my privacy. With this knowledge,
          I didn't think about the phone—since they could
          read my thoughts—and retrieved it, not looking
          at it since they could see through my eyes.
          I slipped it in my underwear and went into
          the bathroom, closing the door.
        </p>

        <p>
          I texted my parents. I emailed my therapist.
          I had never been more scared in my life.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-help-words">
        AN ESCAPE
        <br>WAS IN SIGHT.
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- DIAGNOSIS -->
  <section class="psychosis-diagnosis">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">07 / THE DIAGNOSIS</p>

      <div class="psychosis-diagnosis-heading">
        <span>A FEW DAYS LATER.</span>

        <h2>
          BIPOLAR I.<br>
          <span>WITH PSYCHOTIC</span><br>
          FEATURES.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          A few days later a psychiatrist diagnosed me
          with bipolar disorder type 1 with psychotic
          features. And I finally got some real sleep
          after getting about six hours over five days.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-final-line">
        THAT WASN'T THE END OF IT THOUGH—<br>
        <span>THAT WAS JUST THE BEGINNING.</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- UNDERSTANDING YOUR EXPERIENCE -->
  <section class="psychosis-education" id="psychosis-understanding">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">
        08 / LIVING WITH PSYCHOSIS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT THE<br>
        <span>FUCK JUST</span><br>
        HAPPENED?
      </h2>

      <p class="psychosis-education-intro">
        You experienced something terrifying.
        Something that felt completely real,
        even when the reality you were experiencing
        wasn't the one everyone else was living in.
      </p>

      <article class="psychosis-article">

        <!-- 01 / REALITY -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            01 / YOUR REALITY
          </p>

          <h3>IT WAS REAL TO YOU.</h3>

          <p>
            You weren't sitting around wondering whether
            you were hallucinating. You were trying to
            understand what the hell was happening.
            The voices were real to you. The fear was
            real. The things you saw and felt were
            convincing enough that you reacted to
            them as though they were actually happening.
          </p>

          <p>
            That's one of the cruelest things about
            psychosis. Your mind can create an entire
            explanation for what's happening, and
            you may have no reason, in that moment,
            to question it.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- 02 / HALLUCINATIONS -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            02 / HALLUCINATIONS
          </p>

          <h3>YOUR SENSES TURN AGAINST YOU.</h3>

          <p>
            You heard voices commenting on everything
            you did. You saw faces in the walls and
            curtains moving without wind. You felt
            sensations that seemed to confirm someone
            was hurting you.
          </p>

          <p>
            You couldn't simply decide not to hear
            the voices or see what was happening.
            Your senses were giving you information
            that felt as legitimate as anything
            else you'd ever experienced.
            And you were expected to figure out
            which parts of it to believe.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- 03 / DELUSIONS -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            03 / DELUSIONS
          </p>

          <h3>EVERYTHING HAD AN EXPLANATION.</h3>

          <p>
            You believed you were being watched,
            studied, and punished. You thought
            people could read your thoughts and
            see through your eyes. Even ordinary
            events became evidence that something
            terrible was happening.
          </p>

          <p>
            The frightening part wasn't just
            believing these things. It was how
            everything seemed to fit together.
            The voices, the noises, the movements,
            and your fear all reinforced the same
            story. Questioning it wasn't as simple
            as telling yourself to think logically.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- 04 / BIPOLAR -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            04 / BIPOLAR DISORDER
          </p>

          <h3>THIS WAS MORE THAN A MOOD SWING.</h3>

          <p>
            Psychosis took things far beyond
            feeling unusually energized, depressed,
            or emotionally overwhelmed.
            Your perception of reality itself
            had changed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bipolar I disorder can involve psychosis
            during severe mood episodes. That doesn't
            mean every manic or depressive episode
            will look like this one. But it does mean
            you have to take changes in your sleep,
            thinking, and perception seriously.
          </p>

          <p>
          You're not alone. 63% of people with type 1 have psychosis.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- 05 / SLEEP -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            05 / SLEEP
          </p>

          <h3>THREE DAYS WITHOUT SLEEP.</h3>

          <p>
            You went days without meaningful rest.
            By the time you finally slept, your
            mind had been running through experiences
            that were terrifying, confusing,
            and impossible to escape.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sleep matters enormously when you have
            bipolar disorder. Losing it can be an
            early warning sign of mania and can
            make symptoms worse. You don't have
            to wait until you're hearing voices
            or questioning reality before taking
            serious sleep disruption to your
            psych.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- 06 / AFTERMATH -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            06 / THE AFTERMATH
          </p>

          <h3>AND THEN YOU HAVE TO LIVE WITH IT.</h3>

          <p>
            Eventually, you got a diagnosis.
            You got some sleep. You began to
            understand that what you'd experienced
            had a psychiatric explanation.
            But understanding what happened doesn't
            automatically make the memories less
            disturbing.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can look back and recognize that
            the voices weren't people communicating
            with you, that you weren't being
            tortured, and that the elaborate
            explanations your mind created
            weren't reality. That doesn't change
            how frightening those experiences
            were when you lived through them.         
          </p>

          <p>
          You have watched objects, waiting for them to move. 
          You've listened for voices. You've studied shadows to see if they're
          moving. You've recorded objects with your phone to make sure
          they're sitting still. The memories don't fade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recovery involves more than recognizing
            what was real and what wasn't.
            You also have to understand your
            warning signs, take your treatment
            seriously, and recognize when your
            judgment may no longer be reliable.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need to turn the experience
            into a lesson or pretend it made you
            stronger. You can acknowledge what
            happened, accept that bipolar disorder
            can affect your perception of reality,
            and take practical steps to protect
            yourself if symptoms return.
          </p>

        </section>

      </article>

    </div>
  </section>


 
  <!-- END -->
  <section class="psychosis-end">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-end-small">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ON PSYCHOSIS
      </p>

      <h2>
        REAL TO ME.<br>
        <span>NOT REALITY.</span>
      </h2>

